# The six binary packages of one source package. Every field here was decided
# before it was written; this file is the transcription, and a field it does
# not name is one that was considered and left out. Two of those are worth
# stating as absences rather than leaving to be rediscovered:
#
#   * No Multi-Arch: field on any package. The research came back "not found"
#     for the arch:any-program-with-an-arch-independent-interface case, and a
#     field nobody can cite a rule for is how a manifest acquires cargo cult.
#     Absence means `no`, and the field can be added later.
#   * No ${shlibs:Depends}, anywhere. These are static musl binaries with no
#     dynamic section at all, so dpkg-shlibdeps succeeds and defines no
#     substvar -- naming it yields a silently *empty* Depends field rather than
#     an error. ${misc:Depends} only. dh_installdebconf adds the
#     debconf dependency to it on its own.

Source: kerbridge
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Jarno Elonen <elonen@iki.fi>
# 13, and deliberately not 14. Trixie ships debhelper 13.24 and noble 13.14, so
# compat 13 covers both targets, while compat 14 is in no stable release. 14 is
# also where dh_installsysusers enters the default sequence, and this tree
# creates its unix accounts with the `adduser` call Policy 9.2.2 names instead.
# Re-check the floor with `apt-cache policy debhelper` on the oldest target.
#
# po-debconf is here for `debconf-updatepo`, which debian/rules runs from the
# clean target -- the idiom po-debconf's own documentation recommends, and what
# keeps debian/po/templates.pot from shipping a question set the templates file
# no longer has.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               po-debconf
Rules-Requires-Root: no
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://kerbridge.org/

Package: kerbridge-config
Architecture: any
# admin, not the source stanza's net: this one ships no daemon and talks to no
# network. Same for kerbridge-manage below.
Section: admin
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, adduser
Recommends: ca-certificates
Description: KerBridge configuration tool, and the deployment's shared state
 KerBridge gives cloud-identity users passwordless Kerberos access to on-prem
 services. A sync daemon mirrors the chosen users out of a cloud IdP into a
 local Samba AD domain controller, and a broker exchanges a cloud identity
 proof for a KDC-signed ticket. Workstations join nothing; only the file
 servers do.
 .
 This package holds kbconfig, which validates a deployment's config set, prints
 one value out of it, and writes a new set with your answers already in it. It
 links no LDAP client, so it can run before the realm it configures exists.
 .
 Every other KerBridge package depends on this one. It owns /etc/kerbridge and
 /etc/kerbridge.secrets, it creates the _kerbridge unix group the daemons read
 their credentials through, and it asks the install-time questions the whole
 deployment is seeded from.

Package: kerbridge-issuerd
Architecture: any
# samba-ad-provision holds the LDIF `samba-tool domain provision` reads, and
# samba-ad-dc only *recommends* it: an install made with --no-install-recommends
# has every binary and none of the input, and `kbsetup realm` dies partway
# through the provision. A Depends, therefore, on every supported release.
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         kerbridge-config (= ${binary:Version}),
         samba-ad-dc (>= 2:4.19),
         samba-ad-provision,
         samba-common-bin,
         python3-samba,
         samba-dsdb-modules,
         samba-vfs-modules,
         winbind,
         ldb-tools,
         krb5-user
Recommends: logrotate
Description: KerBridge issuer daemon, and the realm setup command
 KerBridge gives cloud-identity users passwordless Kerberos access to on-prem
 services. A sync daemon mirrors the chosen users out of a cloud IdP into a
 local Samba AD domain controller, and a broker exchanges a cloud identity
 proof for a KDC-signed ticket. Workstations join nothing; only the file
 servers do.
 .
 issuerd is the half of that exchange which holds KDC authority: it runs as
 root on the domain controller, extracts a keytab for one account, obtains a
 ticket-granting ticket with it, and hands the credential cache back over a
 unix socket that only the broker's account may open. It reaches the directory
 only through the local Samba tools, and it opens no network listener of its
 own.
 .
 kbsetup ships here too: it provisions the realm, bootstraps the directory, and
 answers whether durable state still matches the config set -- the last of
 those on the issuer daemon's own start path.
 .
 Install this on the domain controller, which needs a Samba that can provision
 the Windows Server 2016 schema: Debian 13 or Ubuntu 24.04 onwards. KerBridge
 stores every external identity in an attribute that is stock only from that
 schema, and an older Samba ships its definition without offering to lay it
 down. Hence the versioned dependency, which is the whole of the requirement:
 4.19 is the first Samba that provisions the schema, and a release below it
 cannot install this package at all.

Package: kerbridge-broker
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, kerbridge-config (= ${binary:Version}), adduser
Recommends: ca-certificates, logrotate
Description: KerBridge broker daemon, the client-facing half
 KerBridge gives cloud-identity users passwordless Kerberos access to on-prem
 services. A sync daemon mirrors the chosen users out of a cloud IdP into a
 local Samba AD domain controller, and a broker exchanges a cloud identity
 proof for a KDC-signed ticket. Workstations join nothing; only the file
 servers do.
 .
 The broker is the only component a workstation talks to. It validates the
 identity proof against the cloud IdP's published signing keys, decides whether
 that identity is admitted, and asks the issuer for a ticket. It holds no KDC
 authority: everything it can do, it can do because the issuer agreed to it.
 .
 It listens on the loopback interface and is refused any other address in code,
 so a deployment terminates TLS in front of it on the same host. Tested Caddy
 and nginx configurations are in this package's examples directory.

Package: kerbridge-sync
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, kerbridge-config (= ${binary:Version}), adduser
Recommends: ca-certificates, logrotate
Description: KerBridge directory synchronisation daemon
 KerBridge gives cloud-identity users passwordless Kerberos access to on-prem
 services. A sync daemon mirrors the chosen users out of a cloud IdP into a
 local Samba AD domain controller, and a broker exchanges a cloud identity
 proof for a KDC-signed ticket. Workstations join nothing; only the file
 servers do.
 .
 sync is that mirror. It reads the admitted users and their groups from the
 cloud IdP and reconciles one IdP-specific organisational unit per source
 against them: it creates accounts, follows renames, and retires the groups
 that no longer exist upstream. The direction is one-way and the cloud
 directory is never written.
 .
 It is the only KerBridge daemon that changes durable state in the directory,
 which is why it keeps an audit log of its own beside the service log.

Package: kerbridge-manage
Architecture: any
Section: admin
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, kerbridge-config (= ${binary:Version})
Description: KerBridge operator command line
 KerBridge gives cloud-identity users passwordless Kerberos access to on-prem
 services. A sync daemon mirrors the chosen users out of a cloud IdP into a
 local Samba AD domain controller, and a broker exchanges a cloud identity
 proof for a KDC-signed ticket. Workstations join nothing; only the file
 servers do.
 .
 kbmanage manages the resource groups that gate your services, nests the synced
 groups into them, and diagnoses why an identity does or does not reach a
 share. Inside the sync-owned organisational units it reads, deletes, renames a
 login name and revokes a device grant, and nothing else -- a second writer
 racing the reconciliation loop is the failure it exists to avoid.
 .
 It binds over LDAPS and runs as a person rather than as a service, so this
 package installs beside kerbridge-config alone, on an administrator's own
 machine. It creates no unix account and needs none.

Package: kerbridge
Architecture: all
Section: metapackages
# ${source:Version}, not the ${binary:Version} every other stanza uses: this is
# the only Architecture: all package, and a binNMU bumps the arch-any versions
# without bumping this one's -- leaving the metapackage naming a version that no
# longer exists.
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         kerbridge-config (= ${source:Version}),
         kerbridge-issuerd (= ${source:Version}),
         kerbridge-broker (= ${source:Version}),
         kerbridge-sync (= ${source:Version}),
         kerbridge-manage (= ${source:Version})
Description: passwordless Kerberos for cloud identities (metapackage)
 KerBridge gives cloud-identity users passwordless Kerberos access to on-prem
 services. A sync daemon mirrors the chosen users out of a cloud IdP into a
 local Samba AD domain controller, and a broker exchanges a cloud identity
 proof for a KDC-signed ticket. Workstations join nothing; only the file
 servers do.
 .
 This metapackage installs the whole server on one host: the issuer daemon and
 the realm setup command, the broker, the synchronisation daemon, the operator
 command line and the configuration tool. It ships no files of its own.
 .
 A deployment that splits the broker onto a host of its own, or that only wants
 the operator tools on an administrator's machine, installs the individual
 packages instead.
